<h2>How the Packaging Box Volume Calculator works</h2><p>A rectangular box volume is length × width × height. This calculator reports the outside volume and converts cubic centimetres to litres. If a wall thickness is entered, it subtracts twice that thickness from each dimension to estimate a simple internal rectangular volume, making the difference between outside and usable capacity visible.</p><h2>How to use this packaging box volume calculator</h2><p>Enter dimensions consistently in centimetres. Use external dimensions when planning pallet, shelf, carton or courier space. Use known internal dimensions when checking product fit. The wall-thickness field is only a convenience when you have external dimensions and a roughly uniform wall; for corrugated packaging, supplier internal dimensions are preferable.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>Outer volume is useful for cube utilization and storage planning, while internal volume is more relevant to contents. The difference is not literally the volume of board in a folded box because flaps overlap and corners are constructed differently; it is simply the difference between two rectangular envelopes.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>This model assumes a rectangular prism. It does not account for tapered cartons, rounded corners, inserts, dividers, void fill, compression, board caliper changes at folds or manufacturing tolerances. Freight carriers may use dimensional weight rather than physical box volume alone, so use the appropriate shipping calculator for chargeable weight.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>A 40 × 30 × 20 cm carton has a 24-litre outside envelope. If the wall is approximated at 3 mm per side, the internal dimensions shrink slightly and usable rectangular capacity falls below 24 litres. A packaging engineer can compare that with the product’s actual bounding dimensions and protective-fill requirement.</p>
